<div id="container" class="case">
    <p class="crumb"><a href="/nexus">&larr; Back to Nexus platform</a></p>
    <section id="cs-top">
        <h1>Launchpad</h1>
        <h2>A self-serve framework for deploying prototype tools and agents, with governance and a path to production built in, plus the rapid-prototyping practice that fed it.</h2>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-problem">
        <h3>The situation</h3>
        <p>By late 2025, Mod Op had hundreds of people experimenting with generative AI. Good ideas were everywhere: in decks, docs, side projects, and demos that impressed a room and then evaporated. The agency's AI investment risked becoming a collection of disconnected experiments: lots of energy, no portfolio, no way to compare an agent idea against a tool idea against a client-offering idea.</p>
        <p>Ideas were plentiful. Evaluating them was expensive. A business case takes weeks, a deck cannot prove demand, and a full build costs too much to discard when the signal comes back weak.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-role">
        <h3>What I owned</h3>
        <p>I originated and directed the prototyping program across the Q1 2026 hackathons, then led the product and engineering work that turned it into Launchpad. I owned the evaluation model and advancement criteria, including how prototype evidence informed product investment and go-to-market decisions. I designed the platform concept and directed the architecture with engineering.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-insight">
        <h3>The insight</h3>
        <p>The cheapest way to evaluate an AI idea was a rough working version and a one-minute demo. Showing it to colleagues and clients produced useful reactions before larger investment.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-system">
        <h3>How it worked</h3>
        <p><strong>The operating model:</strong> prototype &rarr; scope document &rarr; engineering design &rarr; MVP &rarr; production. Nothing moved forward until a prototype produced strong user or client signal, or an explicit commitment.</p>
        <p><strong>The platform:</strong> a Next.js 15 monorepo hosting 66+ deployable prototype applications at launch. Nexus OAuth 2.0 let them use real single sign-on and inherit the platform's permissions from the start. PostgreSQL tracked engagement, and each prototype included a one-minute demo and structured feedback. A bounty system let anyone in the agency propose an idea and see it picked up.</p>
        <p>The Q1 2026 hackathons produced 60+ working prototypes across infrastructure, subscriptions, delivery, services, and white-label products.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-adoption">
        <h3>Adoption</h3>
        <p>Launchpad became how the agency evaluated AI opportunities. Growth teams used its engagement metrics to decide what to take to clients. The roadmap process shifted from opinion-driven to signal-driven: roughly 100 backlog items were triaged into a ranked portfolio with explicit advancement criteria. GEO/AEO (generative-engine optimization) and Digital Twins emerged from the pipeline as the first priority MVPs.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-commercial">
        <h3>Commercial consequence</h3>
        <p>By August 2026, more than 70 client accounts were in active conversations about Launchpad-derived concepts: GEO, digital twins, competitive intelligence, content and research tools. These were pipeline conversations; no booked revenue was attributed.</p>
        <p>The sharpest proof came from Sparkling Ice and Talking Rain: prototypes built on the client's own brand data, demoed live in the room with product and engineering at the table. Working software beat the capability deck. The session converted into an AI-readiness scoping engagement and became the playbook for how the agency runs major client AI meetings.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-learned">
        <h3>What I learned</h3>
        <p>Demos created shared truth quickly. Building prototypes was easier than enforcing the advancement criteria and killing ideas that failed to earn investment, including some I liked.</p>
    </section>

